## Presets — authoring conventions

Services are declared as **presets** (data over generic providers) and composed in
`infra.config.ts`. See the [`preset-author`](../skills/preset-author/SKILL.md) skill for
the full guide; the strict rules:

### Kind

- `validate` — probe a pasted credential (Resend). Carries `validate()` (also run by `--check`).
- `guide` — dashboard walkthrough + per-env paste collection (Stripe, Google OAuth).
  Reuses the existing collector; do not reimplement masked prompting.
- `managed` — wraps a retained API-orchestrating provider (Neon, Railway, AWS, …) plus
  declarative `outputs`. The heavy client + lifecycle code stays in the retained
  `.provider.ts`.

### Declare outputs (enforced)

Every preset MUST declare an `outputs` array (use `[]` when it emits no env vars). Keys are
`UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`, unique, and must appear in the generated `.env.example`. `guide` fields
must each be a declared output and instructions must be non-empty. The
`preset-lint.unit.test.ts` check fails the build otherwise — never weaken it to pass.

### Naming — single source of truth = `setup.config.json`

Names (org/project/env) come from the loaded config, never hardcoded in a preset. Identity
lives in `setup.config.json`; resources live in `infra.config.ts`.

### Generic providers stay generic

New provisioning shapes go through a generic engine provider (`manual` / `httpApi` /
`generate` / `static` / `command`) — do not add per-service bespoke provider code. If a
shape truly cannot be expressed, add ONE new generic provider (like `command`), not a
one-off. Presets stay data; providers stay generic.

### Chaining

A resource `config` may be `(env, ctx) => ({...})`; `ctx.get('KEY')` reads earlier outputs.
The producing resource must be listed before the consumer in `infra.config.ts`.
